tems-center gap-2 border bg-card px-3 font-mono text-sm text-foreground sm:hidden">menu<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-menu size-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5h16"/><path d="M4 12h16"/><path d="M4 19h16"/></svg></button></div><main><div data-rail="true" class="min-[1152px]:grid min-[1152px]:grid-cols-[48rem_minmax(0,1fr)] min-[1152px]:gap-x-12"><div class="min-w-0"><header class="max-w-3xl pt-8 pb-10 sm:pt-24"><p class="font-mono text-sm text-muted-foreground tabular-nums"><b class="font-medium text-primary">№ <!-- -->33</b> · <!-- -->2026-10-21<!-- --> ·<!-- --> <!-- -->3<!-- --> min ·<!-- --> <a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../server">server</a></p><h1 class="mt-3 max-w-[24ch] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3.5vw+0.8rem,3rem)] leading-[1.15] font-normal tracking-tight">Railway was breached. Every customer has to rotate secrets, and deploys are paused</h1><p class="mt-6 max-w-[52ch] text-xl font-light text-muted-foreground">Railway told every customer to rotate all secrets while deploys are paused. What it has said so far, the first reports of stolen keys, and what I&#x27;d do tonight.</p><div class="mt-10 max-w-md" aria-label="Entries over the past year, with this one marked"><svg class="block h-auto w-full overflow-visible" viewBox="0 0 480 40" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0.0,34.0 L9.2,34.0 L18.5,34.0 L27.7,34.0 L36.9,34.0 L46.2,34.0 L55.4,34.0 L64.6,34.0 L73.8,34.0 L83.1,34.0 L92.3,34.0 L101.5,34.0 L110.8,34.0 L120.0,34.0 L129.2,34.0 L138.5,34.0 L147.7,34.0 L156.9,34.0 L166.2,34.0 L175.4,34.0 L184.6,34.0 L193.8,34.0 L203.1,34.0 L212.3,34.0 L221.5,34.0 L230.8,34.0 L240.0,34.0 L249.2,34.0 L258.5,34.0 L267.7,34.0 L276.9,34.0 L286.2,34.0 L295.4,34.0 L304.6,34.0 L313.8,34.0 L323.1,34.0 L332.3,34.0 L341.5,34.0 L350.8,34.0 L360.0,34.0 L369.2,6.0 L378.5,34.0 L387.7,34.0 L396.9,27.8 L406.2,34.0 L415.4,34.0 L424.6,34.0 L433.8,30.9 L443.1,30.9 L452.3,24.7 L461.5,34.0 L470.8,34.0 L480.0,34.0" class="fill-none stroke-border" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="430.54945054945057" cy="31.99999999999999" r="4" class="fill-primary"/></svg><p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs tracking-wider text-muted-foreground uppercase">you are here<!-- --> · entry <!-- -->33<!-- --> <!-- -->of <!-- -->37<!-- --> · <!-- -->16<!-- --> in <!-- -->the past year</p></div><ul class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground"><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../security">#<!-- -->security</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../deployment">#<!-- -->deployment</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../sre">#<!-- -->sre</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../railway">#<!-- -->railway</a></li></ul></header><article class="prose-ui w-full max-w-3xl pb-10"><p>Today <a href="https://railway.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Railway</a> told every customer to roll all their environment variables, API keys and private registry credentials. The <a href="https://blog.railway.com/p/security-disclosure-wednesday-october-21st" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">same notice</a> says all deployments are paused.</p>
<p>Those two lines don&#x27;t fit together. A Railway service reads a new value when you deploy it, and right now you can&#x27;t deploy. If you rotate a key, your running app keeps the old one until the pause ends, or breaks the moment the old key stops working. If you wait, a key that may be in someone else&#x27;s hands keeps working.</p>
<h2 id="what-railway-has-said">What Railway has said</h2>
<p>Not much yet. An attacker got into Railway&#x27;s systems and may have read customers&#x27; stored secrets. Railway hasn&#x27;t said how they got in, how many projects they touched or which secrets they read, and it has promised more detail later.</p>
<p>For most of the day the <a href="https://status.railway.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">status page</a> described a platform outage with builds failing. It first called this a security incident at 21:59 UTC. The notice went out on X a few minutes earlier, at 21:51.</p>
<h2 id="keys-are-already-being-used">Keys are already being used</h2>
<p>Developers on X and in the <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50188228" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hacker News thread</a> started reporting OpenAI and OpenRouter keys spent by someone else hours before Railway said anything. Others said Anthropic or OpenAI had revoked their keys.</p>
<p>At 21:11 UTC Nevo David shut down Postiz for all its customers. He wrote on X that Anthropic and Stripe had reported its keys as compromised. Postiz runs on Railway, and his post went up 40 minutes before Railway&#x27;s.</p>
<p>So the AI providers and Stripe knew about leaked keys before Railway&#x27;s customers did. I&#x27;d like to know when Railway told them, and why customers heard last.</p>
<h2 id="what-id-do-tonight">What I&#x27;d do tonight</h2>
<p>If you run anything on Railway, I wouldn&#x27;t wait for deploys to come back:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Revoke at the issuer.</strong> Changing a variable in Railway doesn&#x27;t kill the old key. Revoke it at OpenAI, Anthropic, Stripe, AWS or wherever it came from, and accept that the service using it goes down until you can deploy.</li>
<li><strong>Start with keys that spend money.</strong> AI provider keys, payment keys and cloud credentials first, then database passwords and webhook secrets.</li>
<li><strong>Put a spend cap on every AI key</strong> you create to replace them, one key per service.</li>
<li><strong>Check usage since yesterday</strong> on every provider dashboard, and look through your GitHub audit log and the apps that have access to your repositories.</li>
<li><strong>Tell your own users</strong> if a service will be down, on a channel that doesn&#x27;t run on Railway.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then, when deploys return, put the new values in and deploy everything.</p>
<h2 id="after-tonight">After tonight</h2>
<p>Your host holds your secrets and decrypts them on every deploy, so a breach there reaches your keys whatever you do in your own code. This could happen on any platform, and I&#x27;d plan for a day when the keys leak and deploys stop at the same time:</p>
<ul>
<li>Give every AI key a hard spend limit, and give each service its own key.</li>
<li>Give database users the rights they need and no more, and keep databases off the public internet.</li>
<li>Time a full rotation once and write down what breaks.</li>
<li>Know where you&#x27;d run each service if your host froze, and which ones can stay down while you move.</li>
</ul>
<p>Railway says it will publish a full report. The parts I&#x27;m waiting for are how the attacker got in and whether they could read source code as well as variables.</p>
